% ============================================================================
% plant-need — what a plant needs to grow, and the specific role each input
% plays in photosynthesis (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A plant does not simply "eat" like an animal: it builds its own food from
% raw ingredients, and each ingredient does a DIFFERENT job in that process.
% Sunlight excites electrons; water supplies electrons and oxygen; carbon
% dioxide is combined with those electrons to build sugar. Before a learner
% can reason about photosynthesis, they must know WHICH input does WHAT.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? plant_need(sunlight, $Role)      % excites_chlorophyll_electrons
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `plant_need(need, role)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% role WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a
% word that is not one of these three cited inputs (it never invents a role).
%
% Each input and the role the source states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     input             role                          a beginner's cue
%     ---------------   ---------------------------   -------------------------------------
%     sunlight          excites_chlorophyll_electrons  light energizes electrons in the leaf
%     water              split_for_oxygen_and_electrons water is broken apart for its pieces
%     carbon_dioxide     combined_to_make_glucose       CO2 + excited electrons become sugar
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a role and recall the input:
%
%     ? plant_need($Need, combined_to_make_glucose)   % carbon_dioxide
%
% A word that is a REAL plant-growth input but not one of these three named
% roles — `soil` (the source mentions it only as a place nutrients come from,
% with no distinct role sentence of its own) or `moonlight` (not a real
% input at all) — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall on it ABSTAINS
% rather than inventing a role. That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% input→role pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (byte-quotable, per the ADJ-facts standard).
% ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Every row is quoted verbatim from Washington State University's "Ask Dr.
% Universe" — a university science-outreach column that answers real
% kid-submitted science questions — hence `trust consensus` (NOT
% `authoritative`, which is reserved for a primary/official .gov/.edu
% research paper or standards source; this is an outreach column, not a
% primary research publication). Nothing here is asserted from memory. The
% envelope below cites the article's own opening ingredient sentence at its
% `locator`. For full auditability, here is the exact span that grounds EACH
% row, copied character-for-character, from the same article:
%
%   sunlight → excites_chlorophyll_electrons
%     "The light excites electrons in the chlorophyll."
%
%   water → split_for_oxygen_and_electrons
%     "That energy has the power to split water into oxygen and electrons."
%
%   carbon_dioxide → combined_to_make_glucose
%     "Then the excited electrons can jump to the carbon dioxide to make a
%      sugar called glucose."
%
%   https://askdruniverse.wsu.edu/2024/01/23/flowers-use-sunlight-water-grow/
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table plant_need {
    columns need, role

    row (sunlight, excites_chlorophyll_electrons)
    row (water, split_for_oxygen_and_electrons)
    row (carbon_dioxide, combined_to_make_glucose)

    source "The light excites electrons in the chlorophyll."
    locator "https://askdruniverse.wsu.edu/2024/01/23/flowers-use-sunlight-water-grow/"
    trust consensus
}
